---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import Ornament from "../../components/Ornament.astro";
import { withBase, SITE } from "../../lib/site.js";
import { stats, works, quotes, episodes, characters, voices, cuts, glossary } from "../../lib/portal-data.js";

const s = stats();
const readable = works().filter((w) => w.reader);
---

<Layout title="Colophon" description="What this portal is, what it is built from, and how it is licensed." current="">
  <div class="page-head">
    <span class="rubric">Colophon</span>
    <h1>About this portal</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A source-cited reading room for the Matter of Britain, built so that a newcomer can learn the
      famous stories and follow a thread without ever losing track of whose Arthur they are reading.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="narrow narrow--flush">
    <h2>What is here</h2>
    <dl class="facts count-facts">
      <dt>Primary text</dt>
      <dd>{s.units?.toLocaleString()} reading units across {readable.length} works &mdash; about {Math.round((s.words ?? 0) / 1000).toLocaleString()},000 words</dd>
      <dt>Stories</dt>
      <dd>{episodes().length} episodes, retold and cross-referenced</dd>
      <dt>Figures</dt>
      <dd>{characters().length} dossiers</dd>
      <dt>Voices</dt>
      <dd>{voices().length} authors and traditions</dd>
      <dt>Throughlines</dt>
      <dd>{cuts().length} motifs tracked across the whole canon</dd>
      <dt>Quotations</dt>
      <dd>{quotes().length}, every one located in its source by an automated gate</dd>
      <dt>Glossary</dt>
      <dd>{glossary().entries.length} Middle English entries, from the edition&rsquo;s own glossary</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="narrow narrow--flush">
    <h2>Where it came from</h2>
    <p>
      The portal is built on a research base assembled for another project: a quote-validated
      crosswalk of the Matter of Britain across its incompatible versions, with a controlled
      vocabulary, a set of house constraints, and a ledger of validated quotations. The intellectual
      structure of this site &mdash; the seven throughlines, the discipline of naming the version,
      the insistence on counting the cost &mdash; is inherited from that base.
    </p>
    <p>
      What has been added here is the reading text, the retellings, the dossiers, the Grail thread,
      the design system, and the machinery that proves every quotation still says what it claims to.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="narrow narrow--flush">
    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p>
      A static site, generated by Astro, with no server and no runtime data fetching. Public-domain
      source texts are fetched by a checksummed script, transformed into a reading spine by a
      deterministic parser that logs every editorial decision it makes, and wrapped in
      schema-validated JSON. A gate runs on every build: schemas, quotation location, rights tier,
      referential integrity, editorial coverage, text quality, and every internal link.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a quotation cannot be found in its source, the build fails. If an episode has no primary
      passage or no statement of what it costs, the build fails. That is the whole method:
      <a href={withBase("/method/")}>verification is the product</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="narrow narrow--flush">
    <h2>The pictures</h2>
    <p>
      There aren&rsquo;t any. Every mark on this site &mdash; the shields, the ornaments, the
      drop caps, the sword in the favicon &mdash; is drawn in code or set in type. No third-party
      artwork is used anywhere, and no modern adaptation of the legend is named or leaned on.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is partly a rights decision and mostly an editorial one. The visual associations that
      come with modern Arthuriana are extremely strong, and they would quietly become the
      reader&rsquo;s default Arthur &mdash; which is the flattening this whole portal exists to
      resist.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Ornament />

  <section class="narrow narrow--flush">
    <h2>Licence</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Code</strong> &mdash; MIT.</li>
      <li><strong>Original apparatus</strong> (retellings, dossiers, throughline essays, metadata) &mdash; CC BY-SA 4.0.</li>
      <li><strong>Primary texts</strong> &mdash; public domain; every edition and translator is listed at <a href={withBase("/method/sources/")}>sources</a>.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>One exception.</strong> The modern translation of the Vulgate Cycle is in copyright.
        It was used for research and quotation-checking only, is not distributed with this project,
        and appears here solely as two short quotations with the translator named.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small">
      Source code: <a href={SITE.repo}>{SITE.repo.replace("https://", "")}</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .count-facts { font-size: 1rem; gap: var(--s3) var(--s5); }
  .count-facts dt { padding-top: 0.15em; }
</style>
